WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.031 --> 00:00:10.493 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Nigeria's states, part 2 of 2. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:11.155 --> 00:00:21.813 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We work through 17 of the Nigeria regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:22.487 --> 00:00:25.900 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ekiti into view.

4
00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:42.410 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ekiti lies in the south-west of Nigeria, inland and north of Ondo and Osun,
bordered by Kogi, Kwara and Ondo, with Ado Ekiti as its capital.

5
00:00:43.065 --> 00:00:53.131 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from the Yoruba word for hill, and it has produced an unusual
number of professors for its size, so Nigerians call it the fountain of
knowledge.

6
00:00:53.785 --> 00:01:03.340 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the little land of hills, the fountain of knowledge, where two
springs run side by side without mixing at once.

7
00:01:04.079 --> 00:01:05.948 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kogi into view.

8
00:01:12.727 --> 00:01:25.475 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kogi lies in the middle-south of Nigeria, where the Niger and the Benue rivers
meet, bordered by ten other states including Anambra, Delta, Edo and Enugu,
with Idah in the north.

9
00:01:26.126 --> 00:01:37.724 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Lokoja, at the meeting of the two rivers, was the first administrative capital
of colonial Nigeria, and Kogi is the state that touches more neighbours than
any other.

10
00:01:38.383 --> 00:01:45.628 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the place where the two great rivers meet, the old colonial capital,
and the state that touches the most others.

11
00:01:46.309 --> 00:01:48.654 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Osun into view.

12
00:01:53.739 --> 00:02:04.954 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Osun lies in the south-west of Nigeria, north of Ogun and Ondo and south of
Oyo and Kwara, with Ile-Ife in the middle of the forest country.

13
00:02:05.621 --> 00:02:18.880 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Osun-Osogbo sacred grove is a UNESCO world heritage site, one of the last
intact forest shrines of the Yoruba religion, and Ile-Ife is where Yoruba
tradition places the creation of the world.

14
00:02:19.532 --> 00:02:29.377 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the grove of the river goddess and the town where the world was made,
with bronze heads from about 1300 that astonished Europe.

15
00:02:30.053 --> 00:02:32.143 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ogun into view.

16
00:02:37.235 --> 00:02:49.263 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ogun lies in the south-west of Nigeria, between the Atlantic coast and the
inland states, bordered by Lagos, Ondo, Osun and Oyo, with Abeokuta at its
centre.

17
00:02:49.922 --> 00:03:02.287 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Abeokuta means under the rock, and the Olumo rock there sheltered refugees
during the wars of the nineteenth century; the state has produced more heads
of state than any other, including Fela Kuti.

18
00:03:02.948 --> 00:03:09.635 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the rock that sheltered refugees, and the state that has given
Nigeria the most leaders.

19
00:03:10.321 --> 00:03:12.446 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ondo into view.

20
00:03:17.283 --> 00:03:30.797 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ondo lies in the south-west of Nigeria on the Gulf of Guinea, beside Lagos and
Delta, bordered by Edo, Ekiti, Kogi, Ogun and Osun, with the Atlantic on its
southern edge.

21
00:03:31.491 --> 00:03:43.472 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is Nigeria’s largest cocoa producer, and cocoa was the country’s main
export before oil, and the old town of Idanre on its hills is reached by 660
steps.

22
00:03:44.125 --> 00:03:51.846 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the cocoa state on the coast, and the hill town of Idanre that you
climb by six hundred and sixty steps.

23
00:03:52.536 --> 00:03:54.150 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Edo into view.

24
00:04:00.581 --> 00:04:12.563 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Edo lies in the south of Nigeria, inland just north of the coast, bordered by
Anambra, Delta, Kogi and Ondo, with Benin City in the middle.

25
00:04:13.221 --> 00:04:23.972 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Benin Bronzes were looted from its royal palace by a British expedition in
1897 and are now among the most contested objects in European museums.

26
00:04:24.624 --> 00:04:34.690 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the bronzes taken by force in 1897, and the walls of Benin that some
reckon were several times the length of the Great Wall of China.

27
00:04:35.366 --> 00:04:37.154 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Lagos into view.

28
00:04:42.658 --> 00:04:54.605 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Lagos is the smallest state by area in Nigeria, a narrow strip of lagoons and
coast on the Gulf of Guinea west of Ogun, with the city of Lagos spreading
across the water.

29
00:04:55.262 --> 00:05:08.010 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name is Portuguese for lakes, and it was Nigeria’s capital until 1991, and
Nollywood, the second largest film industry in the world by output, is based
here.

30
00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:15.148 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the lagoons on the Atlantic, the smallest state and the biggest city,
where Nigerian films are made.

31
00:05:15.863 --> 00:05:17.906 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Enugu into view.

32
00:05:24.813 --> 00:05:39.952 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Enugu lies in the south-east of Nigeria, on the hills above the eastern Niger
delta, bordered by Abia, Anambra, Benue, Ebonyi and Kogi, with the city of
Enugu in the middle.

33
00:05:40.602 --> 00:05:53.443 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Coal was found here in 1909 and the city grew around it, so it is still called
the coal city, and it was the capital of Biafra at the start of the war of
1967 to 1970.

34
00:05:54.096 --> 00:05:59.170 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the coal city on the hill, the place the war started from.

35
00:05:59.887 --> 00:06:03.033 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Anambra into view.

36
00:06:09.899 --> 00:06:21.881 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Anambra lies in the south-east of Nigeria, on the eastern side of the Niger
river, bordered by Abia, Delta, Edo, Enugu, Imo, Kogi and Rivers, with Awka in
the middle.

37
00:06:22.532 --> 00:06:37.462 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
At Igbo-Ukwu here, bronze castings were made by the lost-wax method from the
ninth century, centuries before Benin or Ife, and the state is known for its
trading and apprenticeship system.

38
00:06:38.117 --> 00:06:44.717 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the lost-wax bronzes older than Benin, and the trading and
apprenticeship system that runs in the markets.

39
00:06:45.438 --> 00:06:47.992 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ebonyi into view.

40
00:06:56.041 --> 00:07:08.243 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ebonyi lies in the south-east of Nigeria, east of the middle belt, bordered by
Abia, Benue, Cross River and Enugu, with Abakaliki in the middle.

41
00:07:08.917 --> 00:07:23.464 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Created in 1996, it is a major rice state and a source of salt, so that
Abakaliki is called the salt of the nation, and its lead and zinc have been
mined since colonial times.

42
00:07:24.129 --> 00:07:31.757 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the salt of the nation in the young state of 1996, growing rice in
the east.

43
00:07:32.528 --> 00:07:35.941 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Cross River into view.

44
00:07:46.895 --> 00:08:00.281 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Cross River lies in the south-east of Nigeria, on the Atlantic coast east of
the Niger delta, bordered by Abia, Akwa Ibom, Benue and Ebonyi, with Calabar
on the estuary.

45
00:08:00.952 --> 00:08:12.934 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named in English for the Cross River, which the Efik called the Oyono,
and its rainforest holds the Cross River gorilla, the rarest great ape in the
world, with about three hundred left.

46
00:08:13.603 --> 00:08:20.975 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the forest with the rarest gorilla in the world, and the old slave
port on the estuary.

47
00:08:21.793 --> 00:08:23.836 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Delta into view.

48
00:08:35.921 --> 00:08:52.047 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Delta lies in the south of Nigeria, where the Niger river splits into a great
fan of creeks and mangrove, bordered by Anambra, Bayelsa, Edo, Kogi, Ondo and
Rivers, with Warri in the west.

49
00:08:52.706 --> 00:09:06.487 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named for the Niger Delta, one of the largest river deltas in the world,
and it produces a large share of Nigeria’s oil, with the pollution and gas
flaring here the subject of international litigation.

50
00:09:07.149 --> 00:09:15.938 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the fan of the river where the oil is, the mangrove creeks and the
gas flares seen from the water.

51
00:09:16.635 --> 00:09:18.597 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Imo into view.

52
00:09:25.368 --> 00:09:36.328 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Imo lies in the south-east of Nigeria, inland between the Niger delta and the
Enugu hills, bordered by Abia, Anambra and Rivers, with Owerri in the middle.

53
00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:49.227 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Women’s War of 1929 spread through this region, when thousands of women
protested colonial taxation and forced a change of policy.

54
00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:59.563 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the women of 1929 who marched against the tax, and the deep gullies
cut into the soil.

55
00:10:00.277 --> 00:10:02.018 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Abia into view.

56
00:10:09.294 --> 00:10:26.651 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Abia lies in the south-east of Nigeria, on the eastern side of the delta,
bordered by Akwa Ibom, Anambra, Cross River, Ebonyi, Enugu, Imo and Rivers,
with Aba in the middle.

57
00:10:27.315 --> 00:10:40.574 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name is an acronym, unusual for a place, made from the initials of Aba,
Bende, Isuikwuato and Afikpo, the districts joined to create it in 1991.

58
00:10:41.232 --> 00:10:50.357 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the only state whose name is an acronym, and the workshops of Aba
making shoes and clothes for all of West Africa.

59
00:10:51.034 --> 00:10:53.031 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Rivers into view.

60
00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:14.283 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Rivers lies in the south of Nigeria, along the creeks where the Niger delta
meets the Atlantic, bordered by Abia, Akwa Ibom, Anambra, Bayelsa, Delta and
Imo, with Port Harcourt in the middle.

61
00:11:14.935 --> 00:11:31.459 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Port Harcourt is the centre of the oil industry and was built by the British
in 1913 to export coal from Enugu, and Ken Saro-Wiwa was executed in 1995 for
leading the Ogoni protests against oil pollution here.

62
00:11:32.122 --> 00:11:38.090 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the oil capital on the creeks, and the man who gave his life for the
Ogoni.

63
00:11:38.765 --> 00:11:41.400 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Akwa Ibom into view.

64
00:11:45.865 --> 00:11:55.884 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Akwa Ibom lies on the Atlantic coast in the far south-east of Nigeria,
bordered by Abia, Cross River and Rivers, with Uyo in the middle.

65
00:11:56.536 --> 00:12:09.667 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from the Qua Iboe river, rendered in Ibibio as Akwa Ibom, it
has one of the highest oil outputs in the country, and its Ibeno beach is one
of the longest sand beaches in West Africa.

66
00:12:10.328 --> 00:12:17.828 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the oil state on the Atlantic with the long sand beach of Ibeno, and
the river that names it.

67
00:12:18.518 --> 00:12:21.711 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Bayelsa into view.

68
00:12:26.089 --> 00:12:37.513 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bayelsa lies in the far south of Nigeria, in the creeks, swamp and mangrove of
the delta, bordered only by Delta and Rivers, and much of it can be reached
only by boat.

69
00:12:38.174 --> 00:12:49.262 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name is an acronym of Brass, Yenagoa and Sagbama, the three areas joined
to make it in 1996, and it is the smallest state by population in the country.

70
00:12:49.916 --> 00:12:57.416 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the boat state, the smallest of them all by people, made from three
areas and reached only by water.

71
00:13:04.101 --> 00:13:21.093 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
17 regions of Nigeria, one for each target of the map: Ekiti, Kogi, Osun,
Ogun, Ondo, Edo, Lagos, Enugu, Anambra, Ebonyi, Cross River, Delta, Imo, Abia,
Rivers, Akwa Ibom and Bayelsa.

72
00:13:21.747 --> 00:13:31.685 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the shape of Nigeria from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
